Crate to Hose Connector

To start with I'm just going to make and test a large rounded rectangle slab with a hole in the center that I can connect to the crate. Bonus points if I can get my idea of putting a thin rubber window-seal type thing in a groove idea working. I don't think an airtight seal is important here but allowing the plate to crate connection be "softer" with some rubber might prevent shaking noise. It may also make it easier to secure, providing some give so the lockring can pull it a little tighter than what the rubber would normally allow relaxed - that way any vibration won't let the ring shake around to its unlocked side and eventual pop off. Maybe??

[crate-connector_v1]
Started with the 24mm hole as always. I'm suspecting I might need to incorporate multiple holes for this. The hose is a 2 inch diameter which means the plate can reasonably cover 2-3 holes easily. It seems a waste to just cover them and not make the airduct direct air through both/all of them. Maybe the plate doesn't really need to be rectangular at all? Just a waste? Make it square to just circumvent the hose? I might test both. It might ultimately depend on whether I can make the air reliably go through multiple holes rather than just creating turbulance and slowing down at the plastic between the holes, reducing the airflow into the crate.

I made the connecting plate as big as the hose connecting part needed to be, which I decided was 70mm diameter currently. I'm likely going to trim this down once I confirm connections work and see what will and won't cover the other vent holes.

The sleeve part the hose can twist into is transparent in this picture. There's two clips to capture the twisting hose wire (not sure what this is called, it's what gives the hose its structure and "ribs"). One on the outer most edge and one in the center. The bottoms of each of these are champfered with 40 degree angles off of vertical so enable printing them without supports....
which *would* matter if I was to pring this thing vertical in the picture, but because of the connector plate this whole thing would need to be on its side. [sigh]

Actually, I realized after typing this that I was planning on having the hose come out the top of the connector, so the hose can go up to the ceiling and around stored gear. I just started modeling the hose sleeve like this straight on for simplicity's sake in the beginning while fitment tests are still in order. So, ultimately that sleeve *will* need overhangs modeled to allow printing without supports. Not wasted time, yay!

I modeled the sleeve before printing the connector plate for testing since I knew the end size of the plate would depend on the sleeve. Now that's completed, though, I'm going to print these pieces separately so I can orient them in the most agreeable way, do the fitment tests, and *then* I'll work on making that turn from coming out horizontal from the box to vertical. And all the consideration of supports that will come with that.

Edit 7/30/26

Okay, with trial and error printing half the collar for sizing tests, I think I'm at the point where the hose will fit nicely into the full collar. There's now two questions to answer:

One - how big should the "plate" part of the connector be and what shape? At bare minimum it just provides a transition point between the crate's vent hole clip and the collar around the hose. It can be bigger and cover up additional close-by vent holes as well. That doesn't currently seem needed from a functional standpoint (will test airflow eventualy to ensure air doesn't just loop right back out them immediately), but even without that it does make a cleaner look. Plus, if it feels a second or third clip would be better for stability those holes can be used.
Ultimately the availability of the extra holes depends on the crate and location - the medium crate on top of the stack has a single rear hole available and is probably the best choice for routing the hose. There's no real reason to have a plate surrounding the hose collar bigger than it. Whereas the side of the medium crates have holes closer together it where it would look clean covering multiple of them. The intermediate and large crates on the bottom will most likely need to be vented into the side holes and those holes are much farther part. The rear of those larger crates also have two vent holes instead of one, so either the connector needs to try to split air into both of them or it'll just need to sit off-center to use one hole and plug the other. Though the rear holes of these crates will be highly unlikely to ever be available with the way the van is packed, but maybe a consideration for a future different vehicle scenario.
[I've attached a few pictures. Good golly I wish I could embed them in here like the Readme. Maybe one day I'll research if that's possible.]

Two - How should I create the connecting points of the two pieces? Why make this in two pieces at all? Well, here's where embedded photos would be nice, but - basically the clipping part that goes into the vent hole really needs to be printed sideways so supports can hold up the clip hooks but nothing *inside* the hooks; however, printing the hose collar on its side means a bunch of supports on the outside and might be impossible to avoid supports from being on the inside. Whereas, if the hose collar is printed with its open end facing up this solves many of those support problems. And I'm able to model the inside "hooks" (what I'm calling the tabs that hold onto the hose's outter thread) so that they have less than the 40 degrees to vertical on the underside to prevent needing supports at all.
The other factor here is that while there's only one hose collar per vent hole clip, I discovered I need to connect TWO hoses to each of the vents and split the air from them. Sadly, there's only a single accessible vent on each side of the back of the van. Other vents are directly in front of the sliding doors, which hit the crates there but make a terrible spot to run a hose from, blocking the door, and then the front center console (actually, is there a rear seat facing center console vents?? I need to check. If so that's another option, but routing the hoses through all the stuff that gets stored there may be the challenge). So, with one hose collar facing out and one facing up, ideally, the orientation of pringing this as one piece gets real squirrelly. So coming up with a connection face for the hose collar that works with both is beneficial regardless. The pieces can be glued together after printing, no need to make fasteners.

So, need to solve the second question first and then can resize the connector plate appropriately for each crate hole.
